<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boso of Provence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 844–<a href="/years/887">887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Boso of Vienne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boso was a Frankish noble who in 879 became the first king outside the Carolingian family since 751. Brother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, he was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> and duke in Provence, and after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and nobles meeting at Mantaille elected him king of Provence, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a>. The Carolingian kings besieged Vienne in 880–82, but he kept part of his realm until his death in 887.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence.jpg" alt="Boso of Provence" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Boson_of_Provence.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boso was the son of Bivin of Gorze, count of Lotharingia, and Richildis, a daughter of Boso the Elder. His maternal aunt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutberga">Teutberga</a> was the wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II of Lotharingia</a>. He succeeded to the lay abbacy of St. Maurice's Abbey in 869. In 870 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> married Boso's sister Richilde, which opened Boso's career in the king's service. That year he was made count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, and in 872 chamberlain to Charles's heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a>, for whom he administered Aquitaine, and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a>.</p><p>At the diet of Pavia in February 876 he became arch-minister and missus dominicus for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and a duke. He was governor and count of Provence from 877 and married Ermengarde, only daughter of Emperor Louis II. In September 878 he accompanied Pope John VIII to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, and the pope adopted him as his son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> died in April 879, leaving two adult sons, Louis III and Carloman II, and both were elected kings. Boso renounced allegiance in July and claimed independence with the title Dei gratia id quod sum, by the grace of God, that which I am. He also claimed that his father-in-law had named him heir. On 15 October 879 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and nobles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> region assembled at the Synod of Mantaille and elected him king, the first non-Carolingian king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> in more than a century. The election was the first free election among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, made without regard to royal descent.</p><p>His realm, usually called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Kingdom of Provence</a>, covered the ecclesiastical provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, Aix, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>. After Louis and Carloman divided their father's realm at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> in March 880, they marched against Boso and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a> and the northern part of his realm. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, they unsuccessfully besieged Vienne from August to November. In August 882 his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-the-justiciar">Richard the Justiciar</a> besieged Vienne again and took the city in September. Boso never regained much of his realm and was confined to the county of Vienne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Boso died in 887 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-blind">Louis the Blind</a>. Historians style him King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> or King of Provence. His kingship failed to win wider recognition and was accepted only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a> and Provence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boso of Provence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder was Richard the Justiciar, count of Autun and brother of <b>Boso of Provence</b>, who gathered several counties under his control, defeated a Viking army near Saint-Florentin in 898 and was recognised as the leading prince of the region, with the ducal title attested by around 918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 879 Count <b>Boso of Vienne</b> was elected king at Mantaille, founding the Kingdom of Lower Burgundy, or Provence, in the Rhône valley; his son Louis the Blind, crowned emperor in 901, was blinded at Verona in 905, and real power passed to his kinsman Hugh of Arles,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 879, after the death of Louis the Stammerer, bishops and nobles meeting at Mantaille elected <b>Boso</b>, a brother-in-law of Charles the Bald, as king of the lands around Vienne, Lyon and Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lower-burgundy">Kingdom of Lower Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote>The region later re-emerged as the Kingdom of Lower Burgundy under <b>Boso</b> in 879.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-provence">Kingdom of Provence</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Vienne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.53,4.88&amp;year=871" aria-label="Vienne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Boso of Provence"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5292 6214h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5292 6214h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.53,4.88&amp;year=871">What was here in 871 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
